{
  "global_card_id": "double_top_double_bottom",
  "global_canonical": "Double Top / Double Bottom",
  "type": "merged_concept",
  "school": "Price Action",
  "aliases": [
    "Double Top and Double Bottom Rejection Pattern",
    "Double rejection pattern",
    "M-pattern",
    "M-shaped pattern",
    "M-shaped reversal pattern",
    "W-pattern",
    "W-shaped pattern",
    "W-shaped reversal pattern"
  ],
  "definition": "A reversal pattern where price tests a level twice, forming an M-shape (double top) or W-shape (double bottom). The second test fails to close beyond the first, signaling momentum loss. Confirmed when price breaks the neckline connecting the intervening swing low (top) or swing high (bottom). Can also function as a trend continuation setup when price breaks through the pattern in the direction of the prevailing trend. The second rejection may act as a liquidity sweep of the first, adding confirmation.",
  "definition_per_source": {
    "SMC-Supplement-PLb0LJvd-CX1wZ": "A reversal pattern where price tests a level twice, forming an M-shape (double top) or W-shape (double bottom). The second test fails to close beyond the first, signaling momentum loss. Confirmed when price breaks the neckline connecting the intervening swing low (top) or swing high (bottom). Can also function as a trend continuation setup when price breaks through the pattern in the direction of the prevailing trend. The second rejection may act as a liquidity sweep of the first, adding confirmation."
  },
  "identification_rules": [
    "Identify two swing highs (double top) or two swing lows (double bottom) at approximately the same price level, forming a clear M-shape or W-shape.",
    "For validation, draw a box between the highest candle close and the wick of the first test (double top) or the lowest candle close and the wick (double bottom); the second test's wick must at least touch the box and the candle body must not close beyond it.",
    "Alternatively, confirm that the wick of the lower test at least hits the body of the higher test, and the body of the higher test does not exceed the wick of the lower test.",
    "Draw the neckline as a horizontal line connecting the low between the two peaks (double top) or the high between the two troughs (double bottom).",
    "The pattern is confirmed only when price breaks and closes beyond the neckline — below for double top, above for double bottom.",
    "Look for confirmation candlestick patterns at the second touch (e.g., engulfing candlestick, momentum candlestick, or long wick rejection) to validate the reversal signal.",
    "The pattern carries higher probability when it forms at a higher time frame key level of structure, supply/demand zone, or area of confluence.",
    "The second rejection acting as a liquidity sweep of the first rejection adds extra confirmation.",
    "After neckline break, apply the Fibonacci retracement tool from the start of the move to the end and identify the 61.8% level as the optimal entry point. If the market extends further before pulling back, adjust the Fibonacci tool to the latest move.",
    "If price reaches the target without pulling back to the 61.8% level, the trade setup is invalidated.",
    "For trend continuation use, the pattern must form within a clearly trending market — a double top in an uptrend or a double bottom in a downtrend — and is confirmed when price breaks through the pattern in the trend direction, followed by a retest of the breakout level."
  ],
  "common_mistakes": [
    "Trading the pattern in isolation without confirming it occurs at a significant higher time frame key level, supply/demand zone, or area of confluence.",
    "Entering immediately after the neckline break without considering a pullback, resulting in an unnecessarily large stop-loss and poor risk-to-reward.",
    "Accepting a second test where the candle body closes beyond the box drawn from the first test's wick to the highest/lowest candle close, which invalidates the pattern.",
    "Trading the pattern without first confirming the higher time frame trend direction (e.g., using the 50 EMA on the 15-minute chart or identifying the prevailing trend).",
    "Waiting only for candlestick rejections at the neckline pullback and missing the trade when no good candlestick formation appears — not considering the Fibonacci 61.8% alternative entry.",
    "Failing to adjust the Fibonacci retracement tool when the market extends beyond the initial move endpoint after the neckline break.",
    "Treating a reaction to an area as a reversal — the pattern must be confirmed by neckline break or additional price action before entry.",
    "Treating the double top/bottom only as a reversal pattern and missing its use as a trend continuation setup.",
    "Not checking higher time frames for nearby structure levels that could limit the trade's potential.",
    "Continuing to look for a pullback entry after price has already reached the target without pulling back.",
    "Not performing backtesting on multiple pairs to build confidence and validate the setup."
  ],
  "trading_implication": "Three entry methods in order of aggressiveness: (1) aggressive entry at the second touch after a confirming candlestick; (2) conservative entry after the neckline break; (3) optimized entry at the 61.8% Fibonacci retracement of the move that broke the neckline, using a limit order. Stop-loss is placed beyond the pattern's swing extreme. Target the next level of market structure, aiming for minimum 1:2 risk-to-reward. Most effective when formed at higher time frame key levels. Also usable as a trend continuation setup after breakout and retest.",
  "merge_notes": null,
